
  [1mThe fork:[0m at the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called, there is no user ID anywhere in the app — the form has only
  username/password, and the ID of "who logged in" is something a real auth API would hand [3mback[0m, not take in. So "add a
  userId parameter" can mean three materially different things, and they leave the codebase in different shapes.

  - [1mCaller-supplied tracking ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m where [38;5;153muserId[39m is a correlation/trace ID the caller
    generates (a UUID, a session key) so you can tie this login attempt to other events. Buys you request correlation
    even for failed logins. Costs: something has to generate it, and it is not the account identity — don't later
    conflate the two. Cheap to change now, painful once other code starts reading it as an account ID.
  - [1mAccount identity, returned not passed[0m — no signature change; the identity comes out in the result ([38;5;153m{ success, user,
[39m    [38;5;153muserId }[39m) and the handler logs it. Buys you the actual "who logged in" answer with no interface change and no caller
    work. Costs: it only exists after a successful login, so you can't correlate failures. This is the shape a real
    auth API gives you.
  - [1mLiteral third parameter, caller passes it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m where the handler reads a [38;5;153muserId[39m the
    form supplies. Buys you exactly what you asked for. Costs: the form doesn't collect one, so this pulls [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
    into the change too, and asking a not-yet-authenticated user to type their own ID is usually a smell.

  [1mMy recommendation: the second.[0m You said the goal is "track who logged in," and the identity of who logged in is
  genuinely output, not input — the first and third options both require the caller to already know the answer the login
  is supposed to produce. If what you actually want is correlating attempts across logs, the first is right and I'd
  take it instead.
